<blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure what emotional impact you're trying to deliver here?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The main emotions behind this are supposed to be sombre and grim. Depicting an originally good deal that goes soar due to the conflict and the inevitable anger as a result. imagine it being like a patriotic mother being told all her sons died in the war and then losing the love of her nation because it sent their children to die.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I would rather envision the USGOV collaborating with the Foundation to perhaps send a diplomatic convoy to initiate peace talks between the ruler and the whoever's sent from the USGOV side.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I imagined that if they tried to send Envoys the Scarkics would slaughter them. Though that could be worked in, as the conflict escalates they try to send in offers of peace terms. They meet with the leaders and are killed as a final message to both the Foundation and the USGOV, this is what pushed the US into nuclear detonation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And then we could send it off on a rather grim/dark note, where the Sarkicists demand something in return, but the USGOV like, cuts them off and says that they never promised anything (or something along those lines) to them during the war, devastating the Sarkicists.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What if after the nuclear detonation, the survivors are so devastated and tired from the war that they ask for peace. Asking for things to return to the ways before, the USGOV refuses and cuts them off as you said. Best of both worlds.</p>

