					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/malycegraves" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4916887); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4916887&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606749136" alt="MalyceGraves" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4916887)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/malycegraves" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4916887); return false;" >MalyceGraves</a></span> <span class="odate time_1587045634 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Apr 2020 14:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4598900">
									

<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">{placeholder}</span></p>
<p>I apologize that it took so long for me to get to this. Work has been super stressful.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>First, from a Sarkic perspective.</p>
<p>It'd be exceptionally weird, from a canonical standpoint, for a proto-sarkic cult to be way out in the middle of the desert, Nevada. They typically shun technology of any kind, and having a group somehow transported from the Urals to Nevada would take some doing.</p>
<p>THAT BEING SAID, I'm sure there are some nifty ways it could have been accomplished, and that might be a fun thing to explore. Also, there is no canon, so why not? Protos are going to be very anti-tech, very isolationist, deeply religious.</p>
<p>Neos would also unlikely to be out in the middle of Nevada. Typically they're very much into high society depravity types, so they don't often stray far from big cities and centers of power.</p>
<p>That being said, I could see an ultramodern cult compound way out in the middle of nowhere, an elite get-away for some high level ultra rich. Also, as Burning Man becomes more and more bougie, it isn't THAT far fetched. Could be an interesting thing to explore.</p>
<p>As to the main bulk of your tale. I think that the difficulty that you're going to run into is the dry aspect of retro-actively documenting all the interesting bits. It can be done, and I've seen it done super well. You've got the Foundation documenting UIU casefiles, so it could be interesting. The nose thumbing by the US and the UIU is kinda neat, and I think it plays right into the way that they've set themselves up.</p>
<p>I see it as the best US operatives have already been picked up by the GOC or the SCPF, and the ones that remain in the UIU are ones that are either so US-first that neither of the other organizations want them, or they've been passed up for recruitment for better candidates. I'm sure that leaves a bitter pill in their mouths.</p>
<p>Having to set off a nuke as a defensive measure is a big deal. Adds some nice flair to the overall idea, but it could also be used as a really poorly crafted trope if you don't put the time and thought into how it plays out.</p>
<p>Regardless, this isn't a bad idea. Hit me up when you have a complete draft, I'm always into reading more Sarkic stuff.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong><span style="color: green">Greenlight</span></strong></p>

